<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chapter-expanding">
<title>Expanding Brackets</title>
<introduction>
  <p>Expanding rewrites a product as a sum of terms. Factorising reverses this process. First learn why every product appears; then choose a clear, efficient method.</p>
  <p>Before you start, check that you can multiply and add signed integers, interpret coefficients and powers of <m>x</m>, and collect like terms. Revisit <xref ref="algebra-vocabulary"/> if you need to revise the language.</p>
</introduction>
<section xml:id="expanding-distribution">
<title>Every term matters: the distributive law</title>
<p>The governing idea is <em>every term in one factor multiplies every term in the other</em>. This is the <term>distributive law</term>. It is the foundation of all the expansion methods in this chapter.</p>
<me>a(b+c)=ab+ac.</me>
<p>Applying distribution twice gives</p>
<me>(a+b)(c+d)=ac+ad+bc+bd.</me>
<example xml:id="expanding-example-distribution">
<title>Multiply every term</title>
<me>(2x+3)(4x+5)=8x^2+10x+12x+15=8x^2+22x+15.</me>
<p>There are four partial products but only three different powers of <m>x</m>, because the middle two are like terms.</p>
</example>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-prerequisites">
<statement><p>Without expanding the brackets, predict the leading coefficient, constant term, and number of partial products in <m>(3x+4)(2x+7)</m>. Explain how you know.</p></statement>
<answer><p>The leading coefficient is <m>3\cdot2=6</m>; the constant term is <m>4\cdot7=28</m>; there are <m>2\cdot2=4</m> partial products.</p></answer>
</exercise>
</section>

<section xml:id="expanding-foil-lab">
<title>FOIL lab: build understanding</title>
<p>In this lab, <em>predict</em> before calculating. Use a different colour to trace each product if you find that helpful. Record your work clearly; large writing and plenty of space are more useful than squeezing the calculation into a small box.</p>
<p>For the product of two binomials, the mnemonic FOIL stands for <em>First, Outer, Inner, Last</em>. It describes four applications of the distributive law. It is not a separate mathematical law.</p>
<example xml:id="expanding-foil-example">
<title>Trace the four products</title>
<p>Use <m>(2x+3)(4x+5)</m>. Predict each product before revealing or calculating it.</p>
<me>\begin{aligned}
\text{First: }&amp;(2x)(4x)=8x^2\\
\text{Outer: }&amp;(2x)(5)=10x\\
\text{Inner: }&amp;(3)(4x)=12x\\
\text{Last: }&amp;(3)(5)=15
\end{aligned}</me>
<p>Collect the two like terms: <m>10x+12x=22x</m>.</p>
</example>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-foil-predict">
<statement><p>Before expanding <m>(3x+2)(5x+4)</m>, predict the First and Last products mentally. Then find Outer and Inner, and collect like terms.</p></statement>
<answer><p>First <m>15x^2</m>, Outer <m>12x</m>, Inner <m>10x</m>, Last <m>8</m>. The result is <m>15x^2+22x+8</m>.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-foil-pairs">
<statement>
<p>Two brackets have two terms each. Write down all the term pairs before calculating anything. Explain how you can tell that none has been missed or counted twice.</p>
<p>Repeat for <m>(2x+3)(4x^2+5x+6)</m>. What changes?</p>
</statement>
<answer><p>The first pair gives four pairs because <m>2\cdot2=4</m>. In the second expression each of the two linear terms pairs with all three quadratic terms, giving <m>2\cdot3=6</m>. List them systematically, one factor at a time.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-foil-limits">
<statement><p>Try to use the four FOIL labels for <m>(2x+3)(x^2+4x+5)</m>. What difficulty arises? How many partial products are required?</p></statement>
<answer><p>There are six partial products, not four. The four-letter mnemonic no longer covers them. General distribution or place-value organisation still works.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<paragraphs xml:id="expanding-foil-reflect"><title>Reflect</title>
<p>FOIL can help you <em>see</em> the four products and avoid omissions. Once you understand distribution, naming all four products can become slower than grouping the middle contributions immediately. Moreover, FOIL does not name all six products when a quadratic factor is present. The important idea to keep is <em>every term by every term</em>, not a four-letter mnemonic.</p>
</paragraphs>
</section>

<section xml:id="expanding-cross-products">
<title>The cross-product method: collect like terms in a margin</title>
<p>For two binomials, a compact alternative is to multiply the leading terms and constant terms directly, then combine the two cross-products for the middle coefficient.</p>
<me>\begin{aligned}
(ax+b)(cx+d)&amp;=acx^2+(ad+bc)x+bd,\\
\text{middle-coefficient margin: }&amp;ad+bc.
\end{aligned}</me>
<example xml:id="expanding-cross-example">
<title>Three coefficients, one margin calculation</title>
<p>Expand <m>(3x+4)(5x+2)</m>. Predict the outside coefficients first.</p>
<me>\begin{aligned}
\text{Leading: }&amp;3\cdot5=15,\\
\text{Margin (cross-products): }&amp;3\cdot2+4\cdot5=6+20=26,\\
\text{Constant: }&amp;4\cdot2=8.
\end{aligned}</me>
<me>(3x+4)(5x+2)=15x^2+26x+8.</me>
<p>The margin collects the contributions to the <m>x</m> column immediately, avoiding an extra line of working. This is still exactly the distributive law; it simply organises the arithmetic more efficiently.</p>
</example>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-cross-predict">
<statement><p>Before writing anything, can you predict the coefficient of <m>x^2</m> and the constant term in <m>(4x+3)(2x+5)</m>? Now put the two cross-products into a small margin and work out the missing coefficient.</p></statement>
<answer><p>Leading coefficient <m>8</m>; constant <m>15</m>; margin <m>4\cdot5+3\cdot2=20+6=26</m>. The result is <m>8x^2+26x+15</m>.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-cross-try">
<statement><p>Use the cross-product margin to expand <m>(2x+5)(3x+4)</m>. Write the leading coefficient, margin calculation and constant term.</p></statement>
<answer><p>Leading <m>6</m>; margin <m>2\cdot4+5\cdot3=8+15=23</m>; constant <m>20</m>. Therefore <m>6x^2+23x+20</m>.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<paragraphs xml:id="expanding-cross-reflect"><title>Reflect</title>
<p>Can you predict the leading and constant coefficients without pen and paper? Which two cross-products must be combined in the margin? Explain why a single middle-coefficient margin is specific to two linear factors. The distributive law itself remains valid for higher-degree factors.</p>
</paragraphs>
</section>

<section xml:id="expanding-place-value">
<title>Place-value multiplication: when a quadratic is involved</title>
<p>For a linear factor multiplied by a quadratic, each term of the linear factor must multiply all three terms of the quadratic. Align partial products under their powers of <m>x</m> and then add coefficients in the same column.</p>
<example xml:id="expanding-place-value-example">
<title>Six partial products</title>
<me>\begin{aligned}
(2x+3)(4x^2+5x+6)
&amp;=8x^3+10x^2+12x+12x^2+15x+18\\
&amp;=8x^3+22x^2+27x+18.
\end{aligned}</me>
<p>Try calculating the bottom constant's full row before moving to the bottom <m>x</m> term. The place-value columns organise terms with the same power of <m>x</m>.</p>
</example>
<p><url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/maths/apps/polynomial-place-value/" visual="Expanding Brackets interactive app"/> lets you work through partial products one by one, group like terms, and reflect on your result.</p>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-leading-constant">
<statement><p>For <m>(3x+5)(2x^2+4x+7)</m>, predict the leading coefficient and constant term first. Then calculate the middle coefficients using aligned partial products.</p></statement>
<answer><p>The leading coefficient is <m>6</m> and the constant term is <m>35</m>. The expansion is <m>6x^3+22x^2+41x+35</m>.</p></answer>
</exercise>
</section>

<section xml:id="expanding-homework">
<title>Homework: consolidate, assess and extend</title>
<p>Complete eight fully worked questions in your Maths copy using the interactive app.</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Select <em>Linear × Linear</em>; complete the four numbered Homework questions.</p></li>
<li><p>Select <em>Linear × Quadratic</em>; complete the four numbered Homework questions.</p></li>
</ol>
<p>You have the whole weekend for these eight questions. Use <em>Step by step</em> to understand and check your working: that support is there to be used. Read the reflection after each question. Then assess your own understanding with quizzes, which give feedback along the way, and tests, which report results at the end after submission.</p>
<p>Practise to develop confidence, enjoyment, accuracy and fluency. A screenshot of a quiz or test result may be shared privately with your teacher if you wish; it is not a substitute for the eight fully worked questions.</p>
<p>You may try extra questions or investigations if you want further challenge. Make a note of any misconception you identify and how you corrected it.</p>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-investigate-common">
<statement><p>Investigate whether expanding <m>(10x+15)(2x^2+3x+4)</m> becomes easier if you first write <m>10x+15=5(2x+3)</m>. Where must the factor <m>5</m> go at the end?</p></statement>
<answer><p>Expand <m>(2x+3)(2x^2+3x+4)=4x^3+12x^2+17x+12</m>, then multiply every coefficient by <m>5</m>, giving <m>20x^3+60x^2+85x+60</m>.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-investigate-negative">
<statement><p>Show that <m>7-3x=-(3x-7)</m>. Explain how this allows a positive leading coefficient inside the bracket and what you must do to the expanded answer at the end.</p></statement>
<answer><p>Multiplying <m>3x-7</m> by <m>-1</m> gives <m>-3x+7</m>. If this expression occurs as a factor in a product, expand using <m>3x-7</m> and then negate every coefficient of the answer.</p></answer>
</exercise>
</section>
<section xml:id="expanding-scaling">
<title>Investigation lab: scaling a factor scales the product</title>
<p>There is one idea behind several useful tricks of the trade: if one factor is multiplied by a number, then the product is multiplied by that same number.</p>
<me>\bigl(kA(x)\bigr)B(x)=A(x)\bigl(kB(x)\bigr)=k\bigl(A(x)B(x)\bigr).</me>
<p>You can scale either factor. More generally, scaling the two factors by <m>r</m> and <m>s</m> scales the answer by <m>rs</m>.</p>
<me>\bigl(rA(x)\bigr)\bigl(sB(x)\bigr)=rs\bigl(A(x)B(x)\bigr).</me>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-scale-double">
<statement>
<p>Expand <m>(2x+3)(x+4)</m>. Now double <em>both</em> coefficients of the linear factor so that it becomes <m>4x+6</m>. Keep the second bracket unchanged and compare the results.</p>
<p>Could you instead have taken out a common factor <m>2</m>, expanded the original brackets and simply doubled every coefficient in the answer? Predict what would happen with a factor of <m>3</m>, or any number <m>k</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer><p>The first product is <m>2x^2+11x+12</m>. The doubled factor gives <m>(4x+6)(x+4)=4x^2+22x+24</m>, exactly twice the first result. In general <m>(kA)B=k(AB)</m>, because multiplication is associative and commutative.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-scale-either-factor">
<statement>
<p>Does it matter <em>which factor</em> you double? Compare <m>(4x+6)(x+4)</m> with <m>(2x+3)(2x+8)</m>. Predict their expansions from <m>(2x+3)(x+4)</m> before multiplying.</p>
<p>Try tripling either factor. Then double the first factor and triple the second. By what number is the original answer multiplied?</p>
</statement>
<answer><p>Both doubled products are <m>4x^2+22x+24</m>. Tripling either factor gives <m>6x^2+33x+36</m>. Doubling one and tripling the other produces six times the original product: <m>12x^2+66x+72</m>.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-scale-hcf">
<statement><p>Before expanding <m>(6x+9)(4x+8)</m>, take out the highest common factor from <em>each</em> bracket. What numerical factor can you place in front of the simpler product? Why might this be efficient?</p></statement>
<answer><p><m>(6x+9)(4x+8)=3(2x+3)\cdot4(x+2)=12(2x+3)(x+2)=24x^2+84x+72</m>. Taking out the numerical factors first reveals the structure and reduces the sizes of the numbers being multiplied.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-scale-negative">
<statement>
<p>Now change the signs of <em>both</em> terms in the factor <m>7x-1</m>, giving <m>-7x+1</m>. Multiply each factor by <m>x+2</m>. Before calculating the second product, predict every coefficient of its answer.</p>
<p>Explain why changing all the signs is the special case <m>k=-1</m> of scaling a factor.</p>
</statement>
<answer><p><m>(7x-1)(x+2)=7x^2+13x-2</m>. Since <m>-7x+1=-(7x-1)</m>, the new product is <m>-7x^2-13x+2</m>, the negative of the original answer.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-scale-common">
<statement><p>Investigate <m>(10x+15)(2x^2+3x+4)</m>. First write <m>10x+15=5(2x+3)</m>. Expand the smaller factors, then scale the answer by <m>5</m>. Is it less work?</p></statement>
<answer><p><m>(2x+3)(2x^2+3x+4)=4x^3+12x^2+17x+12</m>. Therefore the required product is <m>20x^3+60x^2+85x+60</m>.</p></answer>
</exercise>
<paragraphs xml:id="expanding-scaling-reflect"><title>Reflect and connect to factorising</title>
<p>Why does it not matter which factor is doubled? Why must <em>every</em> coefficient of a scaled factor change, not just one? Why does scaling both factors by <m>2</m> multiply the answer by <m>4</m>, not <m>2</m>? Taking out the highest common factor first often simplifies expansion and makes numerical factors explicit, which is especially useful when we begin factorising.</p>
</paragraphs>
</section>

<section xml:id="expanding-hidden-maths">
<title>Hidden Maths: patterns worth discovering</title>
<p>A good investigation begins with a prediction. Look for a pattern in the structure before carrying out all the arithmetic.</p>
<exercise xml:id="expanding-hidden-x-plus-one">
<statement>
<p>Multiply each expression by <m>x+1</m>, but first predict what the coefficients of the answer might be:</p>
<ol>
<li><p><m>(2x+3)(x+1)</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>(2x^2+3x+4)(x+1)</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>(3x^2+5x+2)(x+1)</m></p></li>
</ol>
<p>Investigate: Which coefficients are unchanged? Which come from adding two neighbouring coefficients? How would the pattern change for <m>x-1</m>? Can you describe a rule that works for any polynomial?</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>The answers are <m>2x^2+5x+3</m>, <m>2x^3+5x^2+7x+4</m>, and <m>3x^3+8x^2+7x+2</m>.</p>
<p>Write the coefficients of <m>P(x)</m> in descending powers and imagine a zero at either end. Multiplication by <m>x+1</m> adds each coefficient to the neighbouring coefficient one place to its right. The leading and constant coefficients remain unchanged. Multiplication by <m>x-1</m> instead gives adjacent differences, with the constant term changing sign.</p>
</answer>
</exercise>
<paragraphs xml:id="expanding-hidden-reflect">
<title>Reflect</title>
<p>Could you multiply by <m>x+1</m> by working with the coefficients alone, without repeatedly writing powers of <m>x</m>? Why does the place-value method make this pattern so visible?</p>
</paragraphs>
</section>

</chapter>
